X-ray diffraction (XRD) was employed to analyse the crystalline structure and crystallinity fractions of cellulose and keratin during the dissolution process. Figure  3  presents the typical XRD pattern of the raw hybrid yarn, highlighting the crystalline features of both cellulose and keratin, revealing that the raw yarn’s crystalline structure consists of four distinct peaks. The peaks at approximately 14.8°, 16.7°, and 22.8° refer to cellulose I, the native crystalline form of cellulose [ 53 ]. Similar cellulose I diffraction features and crystallinity analyses have been reported for other natural cellulose fibres, including  Ficus macrocarpa  bark fibres and  Bambusa flexuosa  stem fibres, demonstrating the suitability of XRD for assessing structural organisation and crystalline transformations in plant-derived materials [ 54 ,  55 ]. The prominent peak at 9.4° corresponds to the  α -helix crystalline reflection of keratin, a structural protein in wool [ 49 ]. The deconvolution of the XRD spectrum, where overlapping peaks have been mathematically separated to reveal the crystalline peaks and the amorphous regions, enhance the resolution of individual structural contributions from cellulose and keratin. The deconvolution revealed the  β \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
