The XRD results above suggest that the dissolved keratin fraction is disappearing during the coagulation phase and so could not be used to investigate dissolution. However, previous studies in our group have used the mass loss of this fraction to follow dissolution with time and temperature and so we have used this technique here. There are two ways to follow mass loss of the hybrid yarn and hence follow the dissolution of the keratin fraction of the hybrid yarn: The cross-sectional area ( A ) from direct Optical microscopy and by weighing mass/unit length of fibre. While analysing the changes in dissolution through OM photos, the cross-sectional area of the samples was observed to decrease with increasing time and temperature, and this change is related to the wool dissolution. As we explained in the azimuthal  α  scan, at all temperatures keratin dissolves, and some of the dissolved fibre goes with the liquid during dissolution or washing in water. Figure  7 a represents the changes in the cross-sectional area with time and temperature. We can see that at temperatures between 30 and 50 °C, the yarn shrinks with a ratio of 36–58% after two hours and reached 71% and 77% at 70 °C and 80 °C for the same duration. Again, the significant changes at 70 °C and 80 °C are due to the presence of disulfide bonds in wool keratin. Time–temperature equivalence was used to create the master curves again at 50 °C using the same process as described in Fig.  5 . Figure  7 c shows the rate of dissolution at 50 °C through the decrease of area, as we observed the dissolution is fast in the first three hours due to the IL easily being able to access all the keratin filaments in the outer region. The Arrhenius plot was used to calculate the activation energy, which was determined to be 112 ± 11 kJ/mol (Fig.  7 d). As this is more in line with values obtained for the dissolution of keratin (wool) in [Emim][OAc], we take this as confirmation that following this parameter enables us to follow the dissolution of the keratin part of the hybrid yarns. Figure 7 a  the cross-sectional area of the dissolved hemp – wool yarn in [Emim][OAc] at the given temperatures and time.  b  The master curve plot after shifting to the reference temperature (50 ℃) shown against ln time.  c  The master curve shown against time at 50 ℃.  d  Arrhenius-like behaviour shows the linear relation of  ln a \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
